<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hutsul Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Yasinia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived republic of Hutsul highlanders in the Carpathians, which declared for union with Ukraine after the fall of Austria-Hungary.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In November 1918, as Hungarian rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Transcarpathia</a> collapsed, a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutsuls">Hutsuls</a> in the mountain village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasinia">Yasinia</a> took power locally. In January 1919 it proclaimed its wish to join the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic">Ukrainian People's Republic</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-klochurak">Stepan Klochurak</a> leading the local government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was a small highland territory of shepherds, woodcutters and rafters, run by an elected council and defended by a few hundred armed volunteers. It drove off Hungarian troops and tried to keep in contact with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a> across the mountains, but it had little means of survival on its own.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In June 1919 Romanian troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasinia">Yasinia</a> and ended the republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain</a> then assigned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Transcarpathia</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and the region later formed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carpatho-ukraine">Carpatho-Ukraine</a>. The Hutsul Republic is remembered as an early expression of Ukrainian national feeling in Transcarpathia.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hutsul-republic.jpg" alt="Hutsul Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Ιγκόρ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ukraine_(1917%E2%80%931921).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yasinia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stepan Klochurak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek Catholic and Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hutsul Republic of Yasinia</dd></dl></aside></div>

